00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
i'm under sheriff christopher darcy  and i really wanted to address our  community this afternoon  about the verdict in the derek shawvan  trial today  the death of mr floyd has become a  seminal event in law enforcement  and within our community the chauvin  trial brought up a bigger issue about  american policing  and we're always willing to have that  conversation here at the lvmpd  we've been having this conversation for  nearly a decade  when we began reforms here on our agency  substantive changes are not just  procedural they're cultural  we have worked hard to ensure that there  is no environment  on this police department for an officer  like derek shaubin  many changes have happened over time  with the guidance and input  of community members and representatives  from our metro multicultural  advisory council these members have  really helped us learn  and implement change and have championed  some of our most important policy  changes  in addition we willingly work with these  faith-based partners  community groups and even some of our  critics  i want to say that i am so happy that we  have some of our community partners  here with us today standing over here  to my right mujahideen ramadan  has been working with metro since i was  a very young officer  brought up on this agency and you can  tell that's a long time ago with the  gray hair  but i remember sitting there as like a  brand new cop  and he was teaching us about diversity  and biases and how we can overcome those  by working together  next to mr ramadan we have other members  of our community that  that came to us today and said we would  like to be part of this  we would like to be part of what's going  on with your organization  so i want to thank reverend jackson  pastor gaston reverend wright  and pastor cherry and maria barnes for  taking time out of their day  to come here and be with us so thank you  very much  with our partners we've been able to  start working on recruiting a more  diverse police  officer so our agency will better  reflect the community  that we serve we've been making changes  prior to mr floyd's death metro made  several changes in our use of poly  force policy and training policies  to name a few mandating a recovery  position  making that mandatory so that when we  have a suspect in custody we ensure that  they are breathing and their breathing  is not restricted when they're in  custody  mandating a duty to intervene and a duty  to report  when an officer sees another officer or  a supervisor sees an officer  doing something improper providing more  direction  in what de-escalation really means and  training our folks  on how to de-escalate making that part  of the fabric of everything we do  every call we go on we talk about  de-escalation  we've prohibited choke holds and neck  restraints  we've made changes and we've learned  over time that we have a responsibility  to listen to our community  to you to learn from our community and  to change  the strength of our police department  comes from this community  it comes from you the public among our  many duties to the public  we're here to protect the first  amendment right  to assemble protest some of you may  choose to exercise that right today  and we stand here with you the community  to ensure hand in hand  that they remain peaceful and the  message is heard  this last year has really illustrated a  number  of issues that really impact our  community  and the death of mr floyd was a tragic  event  that should not have happened  having said that today a jury has spoken  and held a police officer accountable  for their conduct  our hope at the lvnpd as we continue to  work collaboratively  and we continue to earn the public's  trust  so i want to thank you all for being  here i want to thank the members of the  community i want to thank my officers  that are here  as well as members of the media and the  public  i am willing to take some questions if  you have any questions of me today  a couple questions talk you notice from  channel 13. i'm  curious going forward will you guys be  using this case the derek show the case  in your training now moving forward well  i will tell you this that's not within  our training  and i think there's not a person on this  planet  that looks at that and thinks that  that's okay so that's never been part of  our training nor will it be part of our  training but yes  obviously the illustration of what  not following policy results  in i think sends a powerful message  yes sir police officer nasa civilian  community member what was your feeling  when you saw that video and what was you  feeling today  when the jury came back guilty  well i will tell you um the day that i  first saw that video i had  another employee who's now a deputy  chief come into my office and said  you have to see this video it's the most  disturbing thing i've ever seen  and he says i think a city is going to  burn and  i looked at it i thought i was just  shocked so it was just utter shock and  disappointment  and then today i would say that the uh  feeling is relief  the yes real victory when it comes to  seeing an officer  held accountable in this country for the  death of an african-american  um also during this trial we've heard a  lot of talk about the blue wall of  silence  what is metro police's reaction to the  conversation that's happened about the  blue wall of silence  and what is being done to make sure that  officers  one thing to you know mandate is to make  sure that officers are actually  reporting  when something happens correct so a  couple things to that  number one we don't tolerate it we  mandate that it does not occur  and we put it in policy but like i said  you have to change a culture so the only  way to change that culture is to enforce  the policy and hold those accountable  that don't follow it  right we train our folks we tell them  what the standard is  we provide them direction on what to do  and how to really attain that standard  and now we have a policy that really  nails it down  and then we follow through and hold them  accountable and we have tracking  mechanisms obviously when we see people  that have complaints we'll hold them  accountable  so i'll take one more question with the  channel eight uh  talk about these relationships with the  community uh community leaders faith  leaders  you know from this point moving forward  how will you continue to have  conversations with them but maybe build  on that further  now that this you know ground swell has  happened over the past year what's  you know what's the big change the big  movement so i think the important thing  is to continue to have the conversation  this provides us a genesis to continue  having conversations about policing in  america and policing in our community  and i think this draws more attention to  the importance of our community  relationships  whether it's our mac council or the i  mean we're involved in thousands of  community partnerships right  every day we have that opportunity to  make a connection with a member of the  public  and i think this serves as a reminder  that we're there to serve the public  and that we work cooperatively with them  and that we continue to make those  changes in law enforcement  the public expects so thank you very  much and then we'll be available for  some questions afterwards i know the  folks from the community will be here as  well so thank you for your time today  i got the mask on right

